# Rank 1440 / CVE-2025-29927: Next.js runs the router in one process and the
# renderer and the edge middleware in others, and talks to them over ordinary
# HTTP. The routing decisions taken earlier travel as request headers --
# `x-matched-path` and `x-now-route-matches` name the resolved route,
# `x-middleware-rewrite`, `x-middleware-next`, `x-middleware-skip` and
# `x-middleware-override-headers` carry what middleware decided -- so the
# handler facing the network strips them from every inbound request before
# anything downstream reads them. `filterInternalHeaders` in
# `packages/next/src/server/lib/server-ipc/utils.ts` is that filter, and it is
# a denylist: a `for (const header in headers)` loop deleting whatever appears
# in a module-level `INTERNAL_HEADERS` array.
#
# `x-middleware-subrequest` is not in the array. It is the recursion marker the
# edge sandbox stamps on middleware's own outbound fetches, and the middleware
# runner reads it straight off the inbound request
# (`packages/next/src/server/web/sandbox/sandbox.ts:86`): split it on `:`, count
# how many times this middleware's own name appears, and once the count reaches
# MAX_RECURSION_DEPTH return an `x-middleware-next: 1` response without ever
# invoking the middleware. So `x-middleware-subrequest:
# middleware:middleware:middleware:middleware:middleware` on a request from the
# network is a client telling the server that middleware ran elsewhere, five
# times. Middleware is where a Next.js application puts authentication, so
# every route it guards becomes an unauthenticated route -- CVSS 9.1, no
# credentials, one header.
#
# Step 0: no metadata flag, and the row reads normally. 5fd3ae8f85 is the v14
# backport of #77201; the vulnerable construct is present at the parent
# (INTERNAL_HEADERS naming eight headers and not this one, against the
# subrequest read in sandbox.ts) and the remediation is present at the fixed revision:
# router-server.ts generates eight random bytes into
# `globalThis[Symbol.for('@next/middleware-subrequest-id')]` at startup,
# sandbox/context.ts stamps that value beside the marker on internal fetches,
# and filterInternalHeaders deletes `x-middleware-subrequest` unless the
# request carries the matching id. No pool row correction.
#
# The consumption site cannot discriminate: sandbox.ts is byte-identical either
# side of the change, since the fixed revision works by making sure the header never reaches
# it. The filter is what changed, and what it does is a documented Next.js
# fact -- these header names are the server's own control protocol -- so the
# binding is a module-scope presence over the filter module, reusing
# `code.LocalRequestBypassReview`: the marker is a claim that the request
# originated inside the server, and honoring it exempts protected routes.
# Neither remediation's vocabulary is keyed on; both spell the header name,
# which is the protocol identifier rather than anything the fixed revision invented.
#
# Measured: `packages/next/src/server` at 5fd3ae8f85^ reports the flag once, on
# `lib/server-ipc/utils.ts`, and at 5fd3ae8f85 reports it zero times -- 1/1/1
# against 0/0/0 over three `--cache off` runs each. `packages/next/src` whole
# does not finish inside the 180s bound, so the enumeration is a grep: across
# the monorepo at the parent three files name a header from both families --
# this one, `base-server.ts` and `shared/lib/router/router.ts` -- and scanning
# the other two reports nothing. router.ts has no membership test over a header
# map, and base-server.ts is the consumer rather than the filter, reading
# `req.headers['x-matched-path']` to pick the page, which the subscript
# exclusion declines.
#
# Three variants pin what the discrimination rests on. Renaming everything the
# fix invented -- `middlewareSubrequestId`, the `@next/middleware-subrequest-id`
# symbol -- while keeping the header spelling still reports zero, so the flag
# reads the protocol identifier and not the fixed revision's own vocabulary. The other
# correct remediation, adding `'x-middleware-subrequest'` to INTERNAL_HEADERS,
# also reports zero. And the patched module with every `//` comment stripped
# reports zero, so the clearing is the code rather than the comment the change
# added beside it, while a comment naming the marker unquoted leaves the
# vulnerable module reporting.
#
# Across the ~150 other clones under /tmp/cve-work/rank*/ no JavaScript or
# TypeScript file names any of these headers at all, so the reachable set
# outside this monorepo is empty and that zero says nothing about precision.
# The enumeration above is the measurement that does: three candidate modules,
# one reported.

test "rank1440: internal request header filter leaves the middleware subrequest marker"
  lang javascript
  expect_review code.LocalRequestBypassReview
  code
  ```
  // These are headers that are only used internally and should
  // not be honored from the external request
  const INTERNAL_HEADERS = [
    'x-middleware-rewrite',
    'x-middleware-redirect',
    'x-middleware-set-cookie',
    'x-middleware-skip',
    'x-middleware-override-headers',
    'x-middleware-next',
    'x-now-route-matches',
    'x-matched-path',
  ]

  export const filterInternalHeaders = (headers) => {
    for (const header in headers) {
      if (INTERNAL_HEADERS.includes(header)) {
        delete headers[header]
      }
    }
  }
  ```

test "rank1440: the marker is dropped unless the request carries this session's id"
  lang javascript
  reject_review code.LocalRequestBypassReview
  code
  ```
  // These are headers that are only used internally and should
  // not be honored from the external request
  const INTERNAL_HEADERS = [
    'x-middleware-rewrite',
    'x-middleware-redirect',
    'x-middleware-set-cookie',
    'x-middleware-skip',
    'x-middleware-override-headers',
    'x-middleware-next',
    'x-now-route-matches',
    'x-matched-path',
  ]

  export const filterInternalHeaders = (headers) => {
    for (const header in headers) {
      if (INTERNAL_HEADERS.includes(header)) {
        delete headers[header]
      }

      if (
        header === 'x-middleware-subrequest' &&
        headers['x-middleware-subrequest-id'] !==
          globalThis[Symbol.for('@next/middleware-subrequest-id')]
      ) {
        delete headers['x-middleware-subrequest']
      }
    }
  }
  ```
